/* data/learn-c/chem-11-08.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/chem-11-08.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-11-08"] = {"14.1":{"s":"Carbon forms more compounds than any other element, from methane to DNA, because its atoms join to each other in chains, rings and networks. Organic compounds are everywhere: in fuels, medicines, plastics, food and living things. This chapter shows how to draw them, group them, name them by IUPAC rules, recognise isomers, and understand how organic reactions happen.","x":["Organic chemistry is the chemistry of carbon compounds. Carbon forms more compounds than any other element, because carbon atoms can join to each other by strong single, double and triple bonds, in long chains, branched chains and rings, and because each carbon forms four bonds.","Organic compounds are everywhere: fuels, plastics, medicines, dyes, food, fabrics and all living things. Most contain carbon and hydrogen, and often oxygen, nitrogen, sulfur or halogens.","This chapter gives the language of organic chemistry. It shows how to draw structures, how to classify compounds by their carbon skeleton and functional group, how to name them by IUPAC rules, how to find isomers, and how the electrons move in organic reactions. These skills are used in all of the organic chemistry of Class 11 and Class 12."],"e":"Why are there millions of carbon compounds? Take only carbon and hydrogen: they can make methane, ethane, propane and so on without end, as chains, branched chains and rings of any length."},"14.2":{"s":"Organic molecules can be drawn in several ways. A structural formula shows all the bonds. The condensed formula leaves out some or all of the bonds, as in CH₃–CH₃. In the bond line (zig-zag) formula, lines are the C–C bonds and carbon and hydrogen atoms on them are not written; heteroatoms and the hydrogens on them are written (OH, NH₂). Three-dimensional drawings use wedge, Fischer, Newman or sawhorse formulae.","w":["In a bond line formula every corner and end is a carbon atom, with enough hydrogens to make four bonds."],"x":["An organic molecule can be shown in several ways. The complete structural formula shows every atom and every bond. The condensed formula leaves out some or all bonds and writes each carbon with its hydrogens: CH₃CH₂OH for ethanol.","The bond-line (zig-zag) formula is the shortest. Each line is a C–C bond. Every corner and every end of a line is a carbon atom, and the hydrogens on carbon atoms are not written, but there are enough of them to give each carbon four bonds. Atoms other than carbon, and the hydrogens joined to them, are written, such as OH, NH₂ or Cl.","Three-dimensional formulas show the shape: the wedge formula uses solid and dashed wedges for bonds towards and away from the viewer, and Fischer, Newman and sawhorse formulas are used for stereochemistry.","Common mistake: forgetting the hydrogen atoms hidden in a bond-line formula. Always count the bonds on each carbon, and add hydrogens until it has four."],"e":"A zig-zag line of three segments, with an OH at one end, is butan-1-ol, CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂OH. It has four carbons: the two ends and the two corners."},"14.3":{"s":"Organic compounds are classified by carbon skeleton and by functional group. By skeleton they are open chain (aliphatic) or cyclic. Cyclic compounds are homocyclic (carbocyclic, with only carbon in the ring) or heterocyclic (with another atom such as N, O or S). Homocyclic ones are alicyclic or aromatic. A functional group is the atom or group that decides the chemical behaviour, such as –OH, –CHO or –COOH. A homologous series differs by a CH₂ unit.","w":["Members of a homologous series differ by CH₂ and have similar chemical properties."],"x":["Organic compounds are classified by their carbon skeleton and by their functional group. By skeleton, they are open-chain (aliphatic) compounds, with straight or branched chains, or closed-chain (cyclic) compounds, with a ring.","Cyclic compounds are homocyclic (carbocyclic), if the ring is made only of carbon atoms, and heterocyclic, if the ring contains another atom such as N, O or S (pyridine, furan). Homocyclic compounds are alicyclic (like cyclohexane, which behaves like open-chain compounds) or aromatic (like benzene, with special stability).","A functional group is an atom or group of atoms that decides the chemical properties of the compound. Examples are the double bond, –OH (alcohols), –CHO (aldehydes), –CO– (ketones), –COOH (acids) and –NH₂ (amines).","A homologous series is a family of compounds with the same functional group, in which each member differs from the next by a CH₂ unit (methane, ethane, propane). Members have similar chemical properties and gradually changing physical properties. Common mistake: calling any ring compound aromatic. Aromatic compounds have benzene-like rings."],"e":"Classify: hexane is open-chain, cyclohexane is alicyclic, benzene is aromatic, and pyridine is heterocyclic. Methanol, ethanol and propan-1-ol form a homologous series, differing by CH₂."},"14.4":{"s":"IUPAC names have three parts: a parent hydrocarbon (the longest chain with the principal functional group), prefixes for branches and other groups, and a suffix for the principal functional group. Number the chain so that the principal functional group gets the lowest locant. Substituents are named as prefixes in alphabetical order, with di-, tri- ignored in the ordering. Many common names such as toluene, aniline and glycerol are still accepted.","w":["Choose the chain that contains the principal functional group first, even if it is not the longest.","Alphabetical order ignores di-, tri- and tetra-."],"x":["An IUPAC name has three parts: a root that shows the number of carbon atoms in the main chain (meth, eth, prop, but, pent, hex), prefixes that name the branches and other groups, and a suffix that shows the principal functional group.","Steps. (1) Choose the main chain: the longest chain that contains the principal functional group, and if there are two of the same length, the one with more branches. (2) Number the chain so that the principal group gets the lowest number, and then so that double bonds and substituents get the lowest numbers. (3) Name each substituent with its number, as a prefix. (4) Write the prefixes in alphabetical order, ignoring di-, tri-, tetra-. (5) Add the suffix: -ol, -al, -one, -oic acid, -amine.","For example, CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃ is 2-methylbutane. The longest chain has four carbons. Two or more equal groups use di-, tri-, and need a number for each: 2,3-dimethylbutane. Some common names are still accepted: toluene, aniline, acetone and glycerol.","Common mistakes: counting the longest chain without including the functional group, and arranging the prefixes by their numbers instead of alphabetically."],"e":"Name CH₃–CH(CH₃)–CH₂–CH₂–OH. The main chain with the –OH has four carbons: butan-1-ol. The methyl group is on carbon 3. The name is 3-methylbutan-1-ol."},"14.5":{"s":"Isomers are compounds with the same molecular formula but different structures. Structural isomers differ in how the atoms are joined: chain isomers (different carbon skeleton), position isomers (functional group at a different place), functional group isomers, metamers (different alkyl groups on each side of a functional group) and tautomers (isomers that change into each other quickly). Stereoisomers have the same bonds but a different arrangement in space.","w":["Same molecular formula is the starting point: C₂H₆O is both ethanol and dimethyl ether (functional group isomers)."],"x":["Isomers are compounds that have the same molecular formula but different structures, and so different properties. Isomerism is of two main kinds, structural and stereo.","Structural isomers differ in the order in which the atoms are joined. Chain isomers have different carbon skeletons (butane and 2-methylpropane). Position isomers have the functional group, or a substituent, at different places (propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol). Functional group isomers have different functional groups (ethanol and dimethyl ether, C₂H₆O). Metamers have different alkyl groups on the two sides of the same functional group (diethyl ether and methyl propyl ether). Tautomers are isomers that quickly change into each other, like the keto and enol forms.","Stereoisomers have the same bonds but a different arrangement in space (geometrical and optical isomers).","To find isomers: write the molecular formula, find the degree of unsaturation, then try all chain skeletons, then move the functional group, and then try other groups. Common mistake: writing the same compound twice by turning a chain around. Check by naming each structure."],"e":"Isomers of C₄H₁₀: butane and 2-methylpropane (chain isomers). Isomers of C₃H₈O: propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol (position isomers), and methoxyethane (functional group isomer)."},"14.6":{"s":"In an organic reaction bonds in the substrate break and new bonds form. Homolytic cleavage gives two free radicals and happens with heat or light in non-polar conditions. Heterolytic cleavage gives a cation and an anion and happens in polar solvents. A carbocation has a sextet of electrons; a carbanion has a lone pair. Reagents are electrophiles (electron seeking) or nucleophiles (nucleus seeking). Inductive, resonance and other electronic effects explain stability and reactivity.","w":["Stability of carbocations and free radicals: tertiary > secondary > primary > methyl.","A nucleophile has a lone pair or is negatively charged; an electrophile lacks electrons (a positive ion or a vacant orbital)."],"x":["In an organic reaction, bonds in the substrate break and new bonds form. A bond can break in two ways. In homolytic cleavage, each atom keeps one electron of the pair, giving two free radicals. It happens with heat or light, in non-polar solvents. In heterolytic cleavage, one atom takes both electrons, giving a positive and a negative ion. It happens in polar solvents.","A carbocation has a positive carbon with only six electrons (a sextet), and a carbanion has a negative carbon with a lone pair. Their stability: carbocations and free radicals follow tertiary more than secondary more than primary more than methyl, because alkyl groups push electrons towards the carbon.","Reagents are electrophiles or nucleophiles. An electrophile seeks electrons: it is a positive ion or has a vacant orbital (H⁺, NO₂⁺, AlCl₃). A nucleophile seeks a positive centre (nucleus): it has a lone pair or a negative charge (OH⁻, CN⁻, NH₃).","Electronic effects explain reactivity. The inductive effect is the shift of electrons along a sigma bond, caused by the electronegativity of an atom or group. The resonance effect is the shift of pi electrons, shown by several structures for the same molecule. These effects decide the stability of ions and the acid strength."],"e":"Compare the stability of the carbocations CH₃⁺, CH₃CH₂⁺ and (CH₃)₃C⁺. The tertiary one is the most stable, as it has three alkyl groups that push electrons to the positive carbon. The order is (CH₃)₃C⁺ greater than CH₃CH₂⁺ greater than CH₃⁺."}};
